KG42.register("rules-90def5fd91a7aeb2b66c7dfc",{"id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R009","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R009","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local clearing price","process_stage":"pricing","market_meaning":"Submitted prosumer net power demands by energy class inform adjustments of the distribution-network energy-class prices. Scope: Distribution-network P2P market; energy-class price adjustments during distributed price-directed optimisation. The platform's update of class-specific net power allocations to prosumers precedes its dual-price update. Scope: Each iteration of the distributed optimisation; intermediate allocation and dual-price updates. The platform agent sets and updates distribution-network energy-class prices and is programmed to set prices that maximise social welfare. Scope: Proposed distribution-network P2P market under its assumed platform and prosumer price-taking operation. Only a single dual price is needed for each energy class at each interval of the time horizon. Scope: Distributed optimisation; each energy class and each interval of the time horizon. Any prosumer's coupling-constraint mismatch may be used to update the dual price. Scope: Distributed optimisation; updates of the common class-specific dual price at each time-horizon interval. The grid-energy class price is above the wholesale energy price, accounting for expected import/export losses. Scope: Reported Fig. 7 case study; distribution-network grid-energy class price. Condition: Power is imported from the main grid. The grid-energy class price is below the wholesale energy price, accounting for expected import/export losses. Scope: Reported Fig. 7 case study; distribution-network grid-energy class price. Condition: Power is exported to the main grid. Green energy trades at or above the grid-energy class price. Scope: Reported Fig. 7 case study; green-energy and grid-energy class prices. Condition: The case-study green prosumers are willing to pay a premium to obtain green energy. Subsidised energy remains at or below the grid-energy class price. Scope: Reported Fig. 7 case study; subsidised-energy and grid-energy class prices. Condition: The case-study philanthropic prosumers obtain additional utility for supplying subsidised energy.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function pricing.local under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["bidding.demand","clearing.local","coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R009-E001","source":"bidding.demand","target":"pricing.local","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R009-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g02-e023"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R009-E002","source":"clearing.local","target":"pricing.local","type":"precedes","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R009-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e024","v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R009-E003","source":"coordination.local_operator","target":"pricing.local","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R009-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E07","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e025"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Distributed optimisation; each energy class and each interval of the time horizon. | Distributed optimisation; updates of the common class-specific dual price at each time-horizon interval. | Distribution-network P2P market; energy-class price adjustments during distributed price-directed optimisation. | Each iteration of the distributed optimisation; intermediate allocation and dual-price updates. | Proposed distribution-network P2P market under its assumed platform and prosumer price-taking operation. | Reported Fig. 7 case study; distribution-network grid-energy class price. | Reported Fig. 7 case study; green-energy and grid-energy class prices. | Reported Fig. 7 case study; subsidised-energy and grid-energy class prices.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E07","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g02-e024","v141-b-g02-e025","v141-b-g02-e030"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-X001","symbol":"\\nu_g^{(t)}(\\kappa)","meaning":"Dual price for the grid energy class at time interval t and iteration kappa; the source interprets the dual price variables as distribution-network energy-class prices.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified by source (dual price variable; no explicit bounds or units given in the quoted text)","unit":null,"indices":["t","κ"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e020","v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-X002","symbol":"\\nu_k^{(t)}(\\kappa)","meaning":"Common dual price for non-grid energy class k at time interval t and iteration kappa; the source states that a single dual price is used for each energy class at each time-horizon interval.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified by source (dual price variable; no explicit bounds or units given in the quoted text)","unit":null,"indices":["k","t","κ"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e020","v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-X003","symbol":"x_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)","meaning":"Net power of grid energy class supplied to prosumer i in iteration kappa, used in the platform agent's update.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified by source (average power quantity; no explicit sign or bound stated in the quoted equation)","unit":null,"indices":["i","t","κ"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e024","v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-X004","symbol":"x_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)","meaning":"Net power of non-grid energy class k supplied to prosumer i in iteration kappa, used in the platform agent's update.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified by source (average power quantity; no explicit sign or bound stated in the quoted equation)","unit":null,"indices":["i","k","t","κ"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e024","v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-X005","symbol":"\\tilde{x}_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)","meaning":"Prosumer i's reported net power demand for the grid energy class in iteration kappa, computed from its local load, renewable and battery decisions.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified by source (average power quantity; no explicit sign or bound stated in the quoted equation)","unit":null,"indices":["i","t","κ"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-X006","symbol":"\\tilde{x}_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)","meaning":"Prosumer i's reported net power demand for non-grid energy class k in iteration kappa, computed from its local load, renewable and battery decisions.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified by source (average power quantity; no explicit sign or bound stated in the quoted equation)","unit":null,"indices":["i","k","t","κ"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g02-e025"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-T001","symbol":"\\rho","meaning":"ADMM step size multiplying the coupling-constraint mismatch in the dual-price update; the source states rho > 0 and reports 0.01 in the case study.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"\\rho>0 (general); 0.01 in reported case study"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e022","v141-b-g02-e025","v141-b-g02-e031"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Submitted prosumer net power demands by energy class inform adjustments of the distribution-network energy-class prices. Scope: Distribution-network P2P market; energy-class price adjustments during distributed price-directed optimisation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g02-e023"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The platform's update of class-specific net power allocations to prosumers precedes its dual-price update. Scope: Each iteration of the distributed optimisation; intermediate allocation and dual-price updates.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e024","v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The platform agent sets and updates distribution-network energy-class prices and is programmed to set prices that maximise social welfare. Scope: Proposed distribution-network P2P market under its assumed platform and prosumer price-taking operation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E07","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Only a single dual price is needed for each energy class at each interval of the time horizon. Scope: Distributed optimisation; each energy class and each interval of the time horizon.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Any prosumer's coupling-constraint mismatch may be used to update the dual price. Scope: Distributed optimisation; updates of the common class-specific dual price at each time-horizon interval.","expression":"\\nu_g^{(t)}(\\kappa)=\\nu_g^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)+\\rho\\left(\\tilde{x}_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right),\\quad t\\in\\mathcal{T};\\quad \\nu_k^{(t)}(\\kappa)=\\nu_k^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)+\\rho\\left(\\tilde{x}_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right),\\quad k\\in\\mathcal{K},\\ t\\in\\mathcal{T}.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-X001","v141-c-R009-X002","v141-c-R009-X003","v141-c-R009-X004","v141-c-R009-X005","v141-c-R009-X006"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R009-T001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e025"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The grid-energy class price is above the wholesale energy price, accounting for expected import/export losses. Scope: Reported Fig. 7 case study; distribution-network grid-energy class price. Condition: Power is imported from the main grid.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e030"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C007","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The grid-energy class price is below the wholesale energy price, accounting for expected import/export losses. Scope: Reported Fig. 7 case study; distribution-network grid-energy class price. Condition: Power is exported to the main grid.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e030"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C008","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Green energy trades at or above the grid-energy class price. Scope: Reported Fig. 7 case study; green-energy and grid-energy class prices. Condition: The case-study green prosumers are willing to pay a premium to obtain green energy.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e030"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C009","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Subsidised energy remains at or below the grid-energy class price. Scope: Reported Fig. 7 case study; subsidised-energy and grid-energy class prices. Condition: The case-study philanthropic prosumers obtain additional utility for supplying subsidised energy.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e030"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Principle of Operation","anchor_text":"y gy The proposed P2P market platform still provides value to these prosumers, by allowing them to t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A platform agent is introduced to act as an auctioneer and to allow energy trading between the prosumers and the wholesale electricity market. The platform agent sets the price of each energy class in the distribution network. A block diagram of the proposed P2P energy market platform is shown in Fig. 2."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Principle of Operation","anchor_text":"Fig. 2. A block diagram of the proposed P2P energy market platform. Distributed price-directed optim","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Distributed price-directed optimisation parallelises the multi-class energy management problem. At each wholesale market trading interval, the prosumers solve local optimisation sub-problems to schedule their renewable sources and battery energy storage systems, considering their generation and load predictions and the price of each class of energy. The platform agent adjusts the energy class prices, considering the prosumer energy demands, the wholesale energy price, wholesale price predictions and expected losses. Iteratively, the prosumers and platform agent reach agreement on a schedule of social welfare maximising power flows."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Principle of Operation","anchor_text":"the current trading interval, the trading interval recedes by a t0 step and the prosumers update the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"It is assumed that the P2P energy market platform operates as a price-taker in the wholesale electricity market. The platform agent is programmed to set prices that maximise social welfare, and the individual prosumers are programmed to operate as competitive price-takers. This is desirable for all participants if the platform owner faces competition from other potential platforms, so it cannot set prices to maximise profits, and there are sufficient prosumers so that they cannot improve their position through strategic price-setting behaviour [33]. If these conditions are not met over the long term, the platform owner/prosumers with market power may need to be compensated to incentivise their participation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e023","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Distributed Price-Directed Optimisation","anchor_text":"k∈K ρ 2 + z(t)ik (κ)−x(t)ik (κ−1)−y(t)ik (κ)−b(t)ik (κ) . 2 ρ > 0 in the penalty terms is the ADMM s","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"2) The prosumers calculate their net power demands for each energy class over the time horizon, and send this information to the platform agent, \\[ \\tilde{x}_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)=z_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-y_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-b_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa),\\quad t\\in\\mathcal T, \\] \\[ \\tilde{x}_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)=z_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-y_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-b_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa),\\quad k\\in\\mathcal K,\\ t\\in\\mathcal T. \\]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Distributed Price-Directed Optimisation","anchor_text":"g an (g ( t ) (κ),x(i kt ) (κ),x(i gt ) (κ)|k∈K,i∈N) R subject to (3), (4), (Power Flow Const.) ‘g L","equation_id":"(22)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3) The platform agent, in parallel for each interval of the time horizon, \\(t\\in\\mathcal T\\), updates the net power of each energy class supplied to each prosumer and the power imported from the grid, for the current iteration, \\(\\kappa\\), by solving, \\[ \\underset{(g^{(t)}(\\kappa),x_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa),x_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)|k\\in\\mathcal K,i\\in\\mathcal N)}{\\text{minimise}}\\quad L_g^{(t)} \\] subject to (3),(4), (Power Flow Const.) \\[ \\begin{aligned} L_g^{(t)}={}&f_g^{(t)}+\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal N}\\left[ \\nu_g^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)\\left(\\tilde{x}_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right) +\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\tilde{x}_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right)^2 \\right. \\\\ &\\left. +\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}\\left( \\nu_k^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)\\left(\\tilde{x}_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right) +\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\tilde{x}_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right)^2 \\right) \\right]. \\end{aligned} \\tag{22} \\]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e025","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Distributed Price-Directed Optimisation","anchor_text":"power balance constraints (3), (4). This means that only a single sy dual price is needed for each c","equation_id":"(23)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4) The platform agent then updates the dual prices. For a particular class of energy \\(k\\in\\mathcal K\\) and time interval \\(t\\in\\mathcal T\\), the prosumer coupling constraints (5), (6), will have the same dual price for all \\(i\\in\\mathcal N\\), since relaxations are interchangeable through the power balance constraints (3), (4). This means that only a single dual price is needed for each class of energy at each interval of the time horizon, and the platform agent can update the prices using the coupling constraint mismatch for any prosumer \\(i\\in\\mathcal N\\), \\[ \\nu_g^{(t)}(\\kappa)=\\nu_g^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)+\\rho\\left(\\tilde{x}_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right),\\quad t\\in\\mathcal T, \\] \\[ \\nu_k^{(t)}(\\kappa)=\\nu_k^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)+\\rho\\left(\\tilde{x}_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right),\\quad k\\in\\mathcal K,\\ t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{23} \\]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Results","anchor_text":"( g gy on management problem is 0.249 s.) e- Fig. 8 shows the net power imported from the main grid,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 7","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"As shown in Fig. 7, the price of grid energy is above the wholesale energy price when power is imported from the main grid, and below the wholesale energy price when power is exported to the main grid, accounting for expected import/export losses. Green energy always trades at or above the price of grid energy, since green prosumers are willing to pay a premium to obtain it. Conversely, subsidised energy remains at or below the price of grid energy, since the philanthropic prosumers obtain additional utility for supplying it."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e020","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Distributed Price-Directed Optimisation","anchor_text":"uses iterative local optimisation and dual price adjustments [41]. The dual price adjustments can be","equation_id":"(20)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"At each wholesale electricity market trading interval, the following distributed mechanism is used to solve the multi-class energy management problem (20). The platform agent initialises the net power of each energy class assigned to the prosumers over the time horizon, \\(x_{ig}^{(t)}(0), x_{ik}^{(t)}(0), k\\in\\mathcal K, t\\in\\mathcal T\\), as well as dual price variables, \\(\\nu_g^{(t)}(0), \\nu_k^{(t)}(0), k\\in\\mathcal K, t\\in\\mathcal T\\), which can be interpreted as the prices of each class of energy in the distribution network for each interval of the time horizon. These initial values are sent to the individual prosumers. Then, during each iteration \\(\\kappa\\): 1) In parallel, each prosumer, \\(i\\in\\mathcal N\\), updates its local decision variables for the current iteration, \\(\\kappa\\), by minimising an augmented Lagrangian, \\(L_i\\), subject to local constraints."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Distributed Price-Directed Optimisation","anchor_text":"4010 IE penalty terms for violations of the coupling constraints. Li = fi+ + ν(t)g (κ−1) z(t)ig (κ)−","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The augmented Lagrangian includes the prosumer cost function, the price of each class of energy over the time horizon and penalty terms for violations of the coupling constraints. \\[ \\begin{aligned} L_i={}&f_i+ \\\\ &\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal T}\\left[ \\nu_g^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)\\left(z_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)-y_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-b_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right) +\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(z_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)-y_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-b_{ig}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right)^2 \\right. \\\\ &\\left. +\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}\\left( \\nu_k^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)\\left(z_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)-y_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-b_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right) +\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(z_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-x_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa-1)-y_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)-b_{ik}^{(t)}(\\kappa)\\right)^2 \\right) \\right]. \\end{aligned} \\] \\(\\rho>0\\) in the penalty terms is the ADMM step size."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e031","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Results","anchor_text":"Fig. 8. The total power imported from the grid, and the total prosumer load power, PV generation and","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"To achieve agreement between the prosumers and the platform agent, the ADMM algorithm is run for 300 iterations at each trading interval, with a step size of \\(\\rho = 0.01\\). The optimisation sub-problems were solved using IBM’s CPLEX solver in MATLAB, on an Intel Core i7-6500U CPU with 8GB of RAM. The maximum solution time is 0.0483s for the prosumer sub-problems, and 0.0395 s for the platform agent sub-problems (the average solution time for the centralised multi-class energy management problem is 0.249 s.)"}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E08","quote":"The platform agent adjusts the energy class prices, considering the prosumer energy demands, the wholesale energy price, wholesale price predictions and expected losses."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e023","quote":"The prosumers calculate their net power demands for each energy class over the time horizon, and send this information to the platform agent,"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e024","quote":"updates the net power of each energy class supplied to each prosumer and the power imported from the grid"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e025","quote":"4) The platform agent then updates the dual prices."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E07","quote":"The platform agent sets the price of each energy class in the distribution network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","quote":"It is assumed that the P2P energy market platform operates as a price-taker in the wholesale electricity market. The platform agent is programmed to set prices that maximise social welfare, and the individual prosumers are programmed to operate as competitive price-takers."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e025","quote":"4) The platform agent then updates the dual prices."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e025","quote":"This means that only a single dual price is needed for each class of energy at each interval of the time horizon"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e025","quote":"the platform agent can update the prices using the coupling constraint mismatch for any prosumer"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","quote":"the price of grid energy is above the wholesale energy price when power is imported from the main grid, and below the wholesale energy price when power is exported to the main grid, accounting for expected import/export losses."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","quote":"the price of grid energy is above the wholesale energy price when power is imported from the main grid, and below the wholesale energy price when power is exported to the main grid, accounting for expected import/export losses."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","quote":"Green energy always trades at or above the price of grid energy, since green prosumers are willing to pay a premium to obtain it."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","quote":"Conversely, subsidised energy remains at or below the price of grid energy, since the philanthropic prosumers obtain additional utility for supplying it."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"609b9d19919e3777bd7562fc574c3774a48245533dcbd26c496cbcfc95b33305","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The supplied source gives an explicit dual-price update for the local clearing price in equation (23). I formalize that equation as C005 using the declared grid/non-grid dual prices, net power allocations, reported net demand, and ADMM step size. The remaining C.1 constraints are procedural sequencing, qualitative welfare-price interpretation, or reported case-study price-order observations without explicit symbolic constraints in the supplied text, so they remain not_formalized rather than being replaced with invented notation. Candidate evidence is unreviewed under this experiment and is not claimed to have passed independent source audit.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-X001","node_ids":["pricing.local","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E003"],"rationale":"The grid-class dual price is updated by the platform agent as the local energy-class price; E003 links the coordinating platform operator to the pricing node."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-X002","node_ids":["pricing.local","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E003"],"rationale":"The non-grid class dual price is the common class-specific price updated by the platform agent; E003 links the operator to local pricing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-X003","node_ids":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E002"],"rationale":"The updated grid-class net power supplied to prosumers belongs to local clearing and is used in the subsequent dual-price update; E002 encodes that precedence."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-X004","node_ids":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E002"],"rationale":"The updated non-grid class net power supplied to prosumers belongs to local clearing and is used in the subsequent dual-price update; E002 encodes that precedence."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-X005","node_ids":["bidding.demand","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E001"],"rationale":"The reported grid-class net demand is the demand-side mismatch input to price adjustment; E001 links demand submission to local pricing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-X006","node_ids":["bidding.demand","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E001"],"rationale":"The reported non-grid class net demand is the demand-side mismatch input to price adjustment; E001 links demand submission to local pricing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-T001","node_ids":["pricing.local","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E003"],"rationale":"The ADMM step size scales the platform agent's dual-price update; E003 links the coordinating operator to local pricing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-C005","node_ids":["pricing.local","bidding.demand","clearing.local","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E001","v141-c-R009-E002","v141-c-R009-E003"],"rationale":"Equation (23) defines the platform agent's local dual-price update from reported prosumer demand (E001), updated net allocation after local clearing (E002), and the platform agent's price-setting role (E003)."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
